# iac/ — AWS ECS Fargate deployment (Terraform)

Deploys {{projectName}} to AWS: ONE production Frappe image (built by
`docker/Dockerfile.prod`) run as five ECS Fargate services —
**frontend** (nginx, the only ALB target), **backend** (gunicorn),
**websocket** (socketio), **worker** (bench worker) and **scheduler**
(bench schedule) — plus **RDS MariaDB**, **ElastiCache Redis** and an
**EFS** file system holding the shared frappe `sites/` volume.

Everything is driven through `dude iac …`. Every command is
**environment-scoped via `--env` (required)**; run any command without
`--env` to list the environments found under `iac/terraform/environments/`.

> **Runner**: terraform runs inside a Docker container built from
> `iac/runner/Dockerfile` (no local terraform install needed). `aws`,
> `docker build/push` and `dude iac login` stay on the host. Override with
> `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to use native tools.

## First deploy, start to finish

```bash
# 0. AWS credentials for the env (profile convention: {{projectName}}-dev)
dude iac login --env dev

# 1. One-time shared backend: S3 state bucket + DynamoDB lock + ECR repo
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --env dev --yes

# 2. Provision the infrastructure
dude iac init --env dev
dude iac plan --env dev
dude iac apply --env dev

# 3. Build + push + deploy the image (tag defaults to the git short SHA)
dude iac ship --env dev

# 4. First time only: configure the bench and create the frappe site
#    (writes common_site_config.json on the EFS volume, then bench new-site
#    against RDS with the admin password from Secrets Manager)
dude iac create-site --env dev

# 5. Check
dude iac status --env dev
dude iac output --env dev        # app_url, db endpoint, secret ARNs, …
```

Log in as `Administrator` with the password stored in Secrets Manager
(`<project>-<env>/admin-password` — see `dude iac output --env dev`).

## Everyday loop

```bash
dude iac ship --env dev          # build + push + deploy
dude iac migrate --env dev       # after any DocType/patch/fixture change
dude iac logs --env dev --service backend --follow
dude iac status --env dev --service worker
dude iac shell --env dev         # terraform/aws toolbox scoped to the env
```

`deploy` records the image tag in `environments/<env>/terraform.tfvars`
(`image_tag = "…"`) — the file is the source of truth for what the env runs,
and a plain `apply` never silently changes the deployed version.

## More environments

```bash
dude iac new-env --env staging          # copies dev's tfvars/backend.hcl
dude iac init --env staging && dude iac apply --env staging
```

The state bucket, lock table and ECR repository are shared across
environments (created once by `bootstrap`); each env has its own state `key`.
Images are promoted between envs by tag: `dude iac deploy --env prod --tag <sha>`.

## Custom domain + HTTPS

Set in `environments/<env>/terraform.tfvars`:

```hcl
domain_name       = "helpdesk.example.com"
route53_zone_name = "example.com"
```

then `dude iac apply`. An ACM certificate is issued and validated via DNS,
HTTP redirects to HTTPS, and an alias record points the domain at the ALB.

## Architecture notes

- **One image, many roles** — the frappe_docker pattern: only the container
  command differs per service. `docker/Dockerfile.prod` bakes frappe{{#if withHelpdesk}},
  Helpdesk{{/if}} and `apps/ticketing` in.
- **`sites/` on EFS** — site config, uploaded files and built assets must be
  shared and durable (Fargate storage is ephemeral). The image keeps a baked
  copy of the assets; the `configurator`/`migrate` one-off tasks sync it onto
  the volume.
- **nginx proxies everything** — the ALB only targets the frontend; frappe's
  nginx config forwards API traffic to `backend.<project>-<env>.internal:8000`
  and `/socket.io` to the websocket service (Cloud Map service discovery).
- **The site name is not the domain** — nginx pins `FRAPPE_SITE_NAME_HEADER`
  to `site_name` (default: the project name), so the same site answers on the
  ALB DNS name or any custom domain.
- **Costs (dev defaults)** — single-AZ `db.t4g.micro` RDS, one
  `cache.t4g.micro` Redis node, five small Fargate tasks, no NAT gateway
  (tasks run in public subnets, locked down by security groups).

## Teardown

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

Destroys the environment; the shared bootstrap (state bucket + lock table +
ECR) is removed only when no other environment still uses it
(`--keep-backend` to force-keep it).
